On 9 May 2006, pendell@hotmail.com wrote:
> xle...@gmail.com wrote:
> > Having recently self-cancelled by accident, and having seen enough
> > posts about BoH accidents, I am starting to think that I will just
> > leave wands of cancellation in my stash.   They can be useful for holy
> > water and such, but I can't think of a time that they ever really
> > helped me in combat.
>
> Gonna disagree ... if you're facing off with a creature with a special
> attack, this shuts down their special attack. Liches can't use touch of
> death or destroy armor,  amulet-thieves can't steal amulet,  nymphs can
> smile at you but can't take anything, werewolves won't give you
> lycanthropy,  vampires won't level-drain you, etc.

The problem is that most monsters get a saving throw against
cancellation, and the more powerful ones that you'd really want to cancel
quickly have better chances of resisting.  Some are effectively
impervious.

If cancellation worked every time against every monster, /oC and
low-enchantment Magicbane would be more popular.

> I *think* the following is also true -- when facing off with
> demogorgan, it disables his disease and stun attacks, making him much
> less trouble. But I'm not certain of that. Suggest wizmode testing
> before trying that in a real game.

The formula for the savings throw is:
   resisted = rn2(100 + alev - dlev) < mtmp->data->mr;
(from zap.c, function resist()).

For a wand, "alev" is 12.  For any demon lord, "dlev" is 50.  For
Demogorgon, "mtmp->data->mr" is 95.  So, we get:

   resisted = rn2(100 + 12 - 50) < 95;

   resisted = rn2(62) < 95;

The result of the random rn2() function is always less than its argument,
so Demogorgon's "saving throw" is actually complete immunity.

(Also, disease attacks do not check cancellation.  So a hypothetical
cancelled big-D would neither drain levels nor cast spells, but he could
still make you Ill.)

